{"timestamp":"2026-10-17T19:09:34.684Z","level":"info","event":"provider.together.response","fields":{"provider":"together","endpoint":"/chat/completions","response":{"httpStatus":200,"contentType":"text/event-stream","providerRequestId":"9c71c972-aws_ew1","model":"moonshotai/Kimi-K3","text":"{\n  \"schemaVersion\": \"outline-v1\",\n  \"targetDurationSeconds\": 180,\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"hook\",\n      \"title\": \"Received, but Also Grown\",\n      \"description\": \"Open by framing the lesson focus: many believers rightly embrace righteousness as God's gift, yet Paul also speaks of a 'harvest of your righteousness.' Pose the tension: if righteousness is a gift, how can it also be a harvest? This lesson resolves that tension.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-7d72-ae3c-1fecbff17080\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"27f538fc-9519-76f8-9022-da2e14ff8e7c\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 20\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"concept\",\n      \"title\": \"Two Manifestations: Gift and Harvest\",\n      \"description\": \"Compare righteousness received as a divine gift (given to all who believe) with righteousness produced as a harvest (on account of obedience). Clarify that the harvest differs in nature, origin, and fruit from the gift, yet the harvest is not a prerequisite to relationship with God but an indistinguishable fruit of it.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-7d72-ae3c-1fecbff17080\", \"01a14b40-adc7-73cf-9e10-2a7998545dd9\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"27f538fc-9519-76f8-9022-da2e14ff8e7c\", \"ddd8a660-bfd1-71fb-bad2-44b3fca1b2c1\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 40\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"example\",\n      \"title\": \"Sowing, Giving, and the Harvest (2 Corinthians 9:6–10)\",\n      \"description\": \"Walk through 2 Corinthians 9:6–10: whoever sows bountifully reaps bountifully; God loves a cheerful giver; He supplies and multiplies seed for sowing and 'will increase the harvest of your righteousness.' Connect sowing and giving to the growth of this harvest.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-72e7-bc33-54f87b9f79f9\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"679469fb-589a-7a5a-9a19-e707aa9eb8b5\", \"ddd8a660-bfd1-71fb-bad2-44b3fca1b2c1\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 35\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"example\",\n      \"title\": \"Vertical vs. Horizontal: David, Goliath, and Saul\",\n      \"description\": \"D","textChunks":["{\n  \"schemaVersion\": \"outline-v1\",\n  \"targetDurationSeconds\": 180,\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"hook\",\n      \"title\": \"Received, but Also Grown\",\n      \"description\": \"Open by framing the lesson focus: many believers rightly embrace righteousness as God's gift, yet Paul also speaks of a 'harvest of your righteousness.' Pose the tension: if righteousness is a gift, how can it also be a harvest? This lesson resolves that tension.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-7d72-ae3c-1fecbff17080\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"27f538fc-9519-76f8-9022-da2e14ff8e7c\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 20\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"concept\",\n      \"title\": \"Two Manifestations: Gift and Harvest\",\n      \"description\": \"Compare righteousness received as a divine gift (given to all who believe) with righteousness produced as a harvest (on account of obedience). Clarify that the harvest differs in nature, origin, and fruit from the gift, yet the harvest is not a prerequisite to relationship with God but an indistinguishable fruit of it.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-7d72-ae3c-1fecbff17080\", \"01a14b40-adc7-73cf-9e10-2a7998545dd9\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"27f538fc-9519-76f8-9022-da2e14ff8e7c\", \"ddd8a660-bfd1-71fb-bad2-44b3fca1b2c1\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 40\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"example\",\n      \"title\": \"Sowing, Giving, and the Harvest (2 Corinthians 9:6–10)\",\n      \"description\": \"Walk through 2 Corinthians 9:6–10: whoever sows bountifully reaps bountifully; God loves a cheerful giver; He supplies and multiplies seed for sowing and 'will increase the harvest of your righteousness.' Connect sowing and giving to the growth of this harvest.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-72e7-bc33-54f87b9f79f9\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"679469fb-589a-7a5a-9a19-e707aa9eb8b5\", \"ddd8a660-bfd1-71fb-bad2-44b3fca1b2c1\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 35\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"example\",\n      \"title\": \"Vertical vs. Horizontal: David, Goliath, and Saul\",\n      \"description\": \"D","istinguish vertical righteousness before God (all human efforts like filthy rags, crowns cast down) from horizontal righteousness before people (faith seen through visible works). Illustrate with David: he spoke covenant privilege to Goliath but presented a practical resume (lion and bear) to King Saul.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-7f25-bd36-d402abf93be9\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"2af4b3fd-3236-7fe7-b029-ff96a9ea899f\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 30\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"concept\",\n      \"title\": \"Tzedakah: Three Dimensions of Righteousness\",\n      \"description\": \"Introduce the Hebrew concept of tzedakah and its three dimensions: personal morality (moral conduct, avoiding sin, personal purity), justice (standing up for the oppressed, illustrated by the Good Samaritan), and charity (being rich toward God and caring for the needy, illustrated by Abraham in James 2:21–23, whose faith was perfected by works).\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-7475-8a3e-66f3a950c7e4\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"ae46abef-303d-74c7-aaed-ae3b93115a06\", \"6353155b-4af1-7602-beb9-ba44a3752dac\", \"54b12e03-243c-7c0a-b6f9-56810778b4e4\", \"08211261-1830-7557-9461-ea0c129d2a58\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 30\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"recall_question\",\n      \"title\": \"Check: Gift, Harvest, and Tzedakah\",\n      \"description\": \"Ask learners: (1) What distinguishes righteousness as a gift from righteousness as a harvest? (2) In 2 Corinthians 9:6–10, what increases the 'harvest of your righteousness'? (3) Name the three dimensions of tzedakah and one illustration of each.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-7d72-ae3c-1fecbff17080\", \"01a14b40-adc7-72e7-bc33-54f87b9f79f9\", \"01a14b40-adc7-7475-8a3e-66f3a950c7e4\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"679469fb-589a-7a5a-9a19-e707aa9eb8b5\", \"ddd8a660-bfd1-71fb-bad2-44b3fca1b2c1\", \"6353155b-4af1-7602-beb9-ba44a3752dac\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 15\n    },\n    {\n      \"kind\": \"summary\",\n      \"title\": \"Work Out What Was Given\",\n      \"descrip","tion\": \"Summarize: righteousness is received as God's gift, yet believers are called to work it out as a harvest through obedience, sowing, and giving. This harvest flows from relationship with God (not a prerequisite to it), is visible horizontally before people, and takes shape in tzedakah—personal morality, justice, and charity.\",\n      \"objectiveIds\": [\"01a14b40-adc7-7d72-ae3c-1fecbff17080\", \"01a14b40-adc7-72e7-bc33-54f87b9f79f9\", \"01a14b40-adc7-73cf-9e10-2a7998545dd9\", \"01a14b40-adc7-7f25-bd36-d402abf93be9\", \"01a14b40-adc7-7475-8a3e-66f3a950c7e4\"],\n      \"sourceBlockIds\": [\"27f538fc-9519-76f8-9022-da2e14ff8e7c\", \"ddd8a660-bfd1-71fb-bad2-44b3fca1b2c1\", \"2af4b3fd-3236-7fe7-b029-ff96a9ea899f\"],\n      \"estimatedSeconds\": 10\n    }\n  ]\n}"],"textChunksTruncated":false,"textCharacters":4746,"reasoningCharacters":0,"finishReason":"stop","inputTokens":"[REDACTED]","outputTokens":"[REDACTED]"},"retries":0,"latencyMs":169351,"service":"pipeline-worker","correlationId":"01a14b42-635f-7cb0-b7ef-75d8b01106b1"}}
